<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salween</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thanlwin, Nu River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salween is a major river of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, rising on the Tibetan Plateau and flowing south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, the Shan and Karenni hills of Myanmar, and along part of the Thai border to the Andaman Sea at Mawlamyine. Its deep, rapid course is largely unnavigable, and for centuries it formed a frontier between Burmese, Shan and Tai states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salween.jpg" alt="Salween" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saosukham · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweet_View_of_Salween_River_in_Tang_Yan_Township,_Shan_State,_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Salween is about 3,289 kilometres long. It rises in the Tanggula Mountains of the central Tibetan Plateau, passes through Cona Lake, and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, where it is called the Nu Jiang, then through Shan, Karenni, Karen and Mon states of Myanmar, where it is the Thanlwin. A short section forms the Myanmar–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> border. Its basin is long and narrow, between the Irrawaddy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> systems on the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> on the east, and about 89 per cent of the annual flow comes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season. Only the last 90 kilometres, at the estuary and delta at Mawlamyine, are navigable. The spelling Salween is an anglicisation of the Burmese name from 19th-century maps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human presence along the upper river dates to at least 31,000 to 39,000 years ago. The Mon migrated south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> around 3000 BCE, the ancestors of the Karen from about 1000 BCE, and Tai peoples, ancestors of the Shan, entered the Shan Hills from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> from about 1000 CE. Martaban was a major port on the Maritime Silk Road as early as 200 BCE, and from 738 to 902 CE the Salween formed the south-western boundary of Nanzhao with the Pyu city-states.</p><p>In the 1060s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> of Pagan conquered Thaton and the Mon kingdoms of the delta, and in the late 1100s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narapatisithu">Narapatisithu</a> brought most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a> under Burmese rule. For almost 500 years the lower Salween defined the frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1824 the Konbaung dynasty ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast south of the river to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and Mawlamyine was capital of British Burma from 1826 until 1852. Teak logs were floated down the Salween to the port. In 1935–36 the British geographer Ronald Kaulback walked the length of the river and produced some of the first comprehensive maps of its course.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Salween is one of the least fragmented large river systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, with only a few small dams on its headwaters and tributaries. Along with the Lancang and Jinsha rivers it flows through the Three Parallel Rivers World Heritage Site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. Its delta is a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing area and its estuary a rich fishery. The basin is home to many minority peoples, including the Karen, Shan, Mon and Nu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salween River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Karenni chiefs, called saopha in the Shan style, ruled over rice farmers, hill cultivators and teak forests, and traded and raided across the <b>Salween</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/karenni-states">Karenni States</a></li><li><blockquote>South Hsenwi was ruled by a hereditary saopha under British supervision, in a region of Shan farmers and hill peoples on the routes between the <b>Salween</b> and the plateau.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-hsenwi">South Hsenwi</a></li></ul></section>
